# Line-ending and diff normalisation.
#
# Without this, the bytes a contributor sees depend on their `core.autocrlf`
# setting: the same commit checks out as LF on Linux and CRLF on Windows. Two
# things in this repository are sensitive to that:
#
#   - the acceptance run records a hash of `package-lock.json` as part of its
#     evidence, and two machines would record different hashes for the same
#     commit; and
#   - the release build asserts that exactly one inline script is hashed into
#     its CSP, and a hash taken over CRLF bytes is not the hash the browser
#     computes over the LF bytes it receives.
#
# So: text files are stored and checked out as LF everywhere. The CSP assertion
# also normalises CRLF defensively, because a `git archive` or an editor can
# still hand it CRLF, but with this file in place that path should not be
# reached.
#
# If your working copy was checked out before this file existed it may still
# hold CRLF; `git add --renormalize .` rewrites the index to the normalised
# form, and a fresh clone gets LF directly.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary. `text=auto` usually detects these, but naming them means a
# future file in one of these formats cannot be silently mangled by a
# line-ending conversion.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.pdf binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.wasm binary
*.mp4 binary

# GitHub language statistics: the generated third-party notice texts are not
# this repository's source, and the lockfile is generated. Marking them here
# affects only how a repository host counts languages — not how git shows a
# diff for them.
package-lock.json linguist-generated
licenses/** linguist-vendored

# Preserve third-party notice bytes and the official license verbatim.
licenses/** -text -whitespace
LICENSE -text
